Exercise 1.25 asks what \(|\mathcal{D}|\) is when \(\mathcal{D}\) is the family of nonempty final segments of a well-ordered set \(\Delta\), whether every element is finite, and whether every approximation to a finite element is finite. `finalSegmentSystem` is that family on a nonempty linear order, the nonempty upper sets, and it is a neighbourhood system because `upperSet_subset_or` nests any two of them and supplies the nested-or-disjoint law; `mem_def`, `master_eq`, and `Ici_mem` record membership, the master as the whole token set, and the principal final segments. On a well-order, `exists_Ici_of_mem` shows that every neighbourhood is the principal final segment of its least element. `lowerSetOf` is the set of tokens whose principal final segment lies in a filter, `lowerSetOf_isLowerSet` and `lowerSetOf_nonempty` make that trace a nonempty lower set, and `ofLowerSet` rebuilds the filter of neighbourhoods containing the principal final segment of some member, using the maximum of two witnesses for the intersection law; `lowerSetOf_ofLowerSet` and `ofLowerSet_lowerSetOf` are the round trips, and `finalSegmentClassify` is the order isomorphism of the domain with the nonempty lower sets ordered by inclusion, so one filter approximates another exactly when its lower set is contained in the other. `topElement` is the filter of every neighbourhood; `le_topElement`, `topElement_isTotal`, and `eq_topElement_of_isTotal` make it the unique total element, and `topElement_not_principal_of_noMax` shows that if \(\Delta\) has no greatest element then this total element differs from \(\uparrow(\mathrm{Ici}\,a)\) for every \(a\), so not every element is finite. `principal_Ici_le_iff` orders the finite elements by \(\uparrow(\mathrm{Ici}\,a)\le\uparrow(\mathrm{Ici}\,b)\) if and only if \(a\le b\). The question whether every approximation to a finite element is finite is not answered. **Theorem:** partial — the domain is the poset of nonempty lower sets, and without a greatest token not every element is finite, while the approximation question stays open. **Proof:** partial — the nested-upper-set system, the least-element representation, the two round trips, and the separation of the top element from every principal filter are proved; there is no argument about elements lying under a finite element.
